#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Wave 36: Little Hands, Just Three, a Seventh Time — the seventh 3-only wave.

Wave 35's honest assessment named this wave the same way waves
29-34 named theirs: the age-3 gap (130 against 160 at age 4, gap
30) "closes only by more waves of this kind, at honest quality,
or not at all." Six consecutive 3-only waves have narrowed it
102 -> 90 -> 78 -> 66 -> 54 -> 42 -> 30. This is the seventh:
twelve more records banded 3-3, built to the rules waves 30-35
proved — short (<= 20 minutes), adult-led, big pieces, one rule
per game, the adult as a player — because the band is honest for
the same reason: these are three-year-old moments (a red leaf
held up to the light with the sun shining through it like a
stained-glass window, one hand's warmth lent to a cold one until
both hands agree, a bell rung for the one person across the room
whose name it carries), and age 4, holding 160 records, does not
miss them.

Measured before this wave (primary category = categories[0],
records covering age 3): holidays and team-community were the
floor at 10 each; seasons / stem / icebreakers / outdoor 11
each; crafts 12, pe-movement 12, rainy-day 16, quiet-focus 15.
Music-rhythm (36) is now the thinnest primary category on the
whole shelf, rainy-day (37) second. So the pack repeats the
proven shape exactly: one record for every one of the 11
categories — the floor rises 10 -> 11 everywhere — plus a
second music-rhythm record. Result, asserted in the wave log:
age 3 130 -> 142, age 4 unchanged at 160, the headline gap
30 -> 18, narrowed for the seventh wave running. Tag deltas:
Indigenous Peoples' Day 5 -> 6 (the last holiday tag on the
floor of 5 rises), spring 40 -> 41 (the second-thinnest season
rises).

The drama/coding lane, recorded not padded (standing rule):
there is still no drama or coding category in the archive's
eleven, and no tag in the validator's vocabulary names one.
This wave does not invent a twelfth category to look complete,
and it does not stretch a story-circle or a pattern game into a
'drama' or 'coding' label it is not. The lane stays on the
record as an honest gap until a wave is briefed to build it
properly, with its own category, its own shelf, and records
written for it first.

Near-duplicate screen: token Jaccard >= 0.55 against every
existing name+tagline is asserted below and fails the run.
Closest deliberate neighbours, kept on purpose:
- big-leaf-light-window-hold vs leaf-collect-and-sort (older
  kids gathering and classifying many leaves): nothing is
  collected or sorted here — ONE leaf, chosen by its holder,
  is held to the light and looked THROUGH, and the looking,
  not the leaf pile, is the record.
- big-paper-fan-wind-maker vs big-wind-sock-weather-watch
  (3-only, wave 28 shelf: a sock watched to read the wind):
  the sock there is READ; the fan here is WORKED — the wind
  is made by small arms, aimed, and felt on faces, and the
  still moment when the wind stops is the lesson.
- big-tissue-snow-gentle-fall vs indoor-snowball-toss (older
  kids, pom-pom snowballs thrown at bucket targets): nothing
  is thrown and nothing is scored here — the snow FALLS, one
  float at a time, and the group's whole work is gentleness.
- big-float-or-sink-first-dip vs big-float-sink-basin-sort
  (screen's closest at 0.26 — a sorting game: objects tested
  and sorted into float/sink basins as the play itself): no
  sorting and no basins here — each object is dipped ONCE,
  ceremonially, reported in the session's two words only,
  and retired to its shore row; the sponge that changes its
  answer mid-watch is this record's centre, and sorting it
  would end the watching that is the whole point.
- big-warm-hand-lend-a-hand vs warm-up tag games on the
  movement shelf: no chasing, no tagging, no it — two hands
  meet palm to palm by invitation, and the lending of warmth
  is the whole event; the game's only score is agreement.
- big-find-the-matching-sock-pair vs laundry-sort relays
  (older kids racing baskets): no race and no baskets — one
  sock is held up, its ONE partner is found from a small
  pile by eye and by hand, and a found pair is celebrated
  at full size however long the finding took.
- big-name-bell-ring-for-you vs name-game rhythm records on
  the music shelf (names chanted and clapped in patterns):
  no pattern is kept here and no chant is built — one bell
  rings for one name, the named child answers or doesn't,
  and listening FOR your own name is the entire instrument.
- big-tear-and-stick-paper-mosaic vs torn-paper-collage
  (screen's closest at 0.21 — an individual collage at the
  older shelf): that record is one child, one picture, their
  own composition; this one is a SHARED outline so large ten
  makers work it at once, pieces palm-sized by law, and the
  craft taught is tearing itself — thumbs close, rip slow —
  as a pre-scissor skill at three, not composition.
- big-shoe-stomp-marching-band vs marching and parade
  records for older kids (routes, formations, an audience):
  no route and no formation here — the band marches ON THE
  SPOT, shakers in hand, and the conductor's raised hand,
  not a finish line, ends the music.
- big-ribbon-wand-river-dance vs big-ribbon-wand-parade-walk
  (screen's closest at 0.21 — wands carried in a walking
  parade): the parade there TRAVELS and is watched; here
  nobody goes anywhere and nobody watches — dancers stand
  in their own turning circles on the quiet-focus shelf,
  the music conducts, and the record's last movement is a
  held silence after the final note.
- big-cushion-bridge-crossing vs obstacle-course records
  (many stations, timed or themed runs): one crossing, one
  cushion bridge, walked at the crosser's own pace with an
  adult hand offered, never required — arriving is the
  whole course, and nobody's time is anybody's business.
- big-big-button-press-and-pop vs button and zipper boards
  on the fine-motor shelf (dressing frames worked solo):
  the board there is furniture; the buttons here are worn
  — one huge button on the adult's cardigan, pressed
  through its hole by each visitor in turn, and the POP
  of its arrival is a small ceremony, shared.

Safety notes are practical only — no medical claims anywhere.

Run once: dedupes by id, writes data/activities.json + collections.json.
Run scripts/build.py afterwards.
"""
